we got genuinely bad jobs news from  February as we often do in the first  part of each month we get the jobs  numbers from the previous month 180,000  new jobs were expected for February of  2019 at the actual number just twenty  thousand new jobs  180 expected twenty actual this is 89%  shy of the expected number still jobs  were created so in a sense that's good  it's better to create jobs than to lose  jobs but remember that the population  expands the number of people who enter  the potential labor force goes up each  month so you need some new jobs just to  keep up with population growth and that  number depending on which estimates you  look at is ninety to a hundred and  forty-five thousand new jobs just to  keep up with new people that are of  working age so twenty thousand new jobs  is actually quite bad now just like I  did under Obama  I don't play these partisan games where  I either sugarcoat or exclude  information that's positive or negative  on the basis of the party of the person  in the White House I'm not doing that  the same way that under Obama I made an  effort not to over or understate the  importance of any one number I'm also  going to be totally upfront with you  that this is not a clear trend under the  trump administration yet there's some  wage growth not much more than inflation  but there is some wage growth that's  good the unemployment rate is still low  that's good although we're starting to  sort of learn about what the the sort of  other side of low unemployment it can be  in terms of a very difficult labor  market for hiring people but when you  combine a job creation number that's  eighty nine percent lower than expected  with a newly downgraded estimate for GDP  growth for this year which we just  received that's not looking great I  think anyone who is honestly assessing  the numbers would admit now that being  said not everything that happens now is  because of trump good or bad there's no  sort of like one point at which we can  say okay  from now on everything that happens in  the economy everything that happens with  jobs everything that happens with the  stock market it's now only attributable  to Trump or it's still attributable to  the previous president this is very  complicated stuff over a full term of a  president you can probably start to say  that most of what we're seeing is more  than likely the result of the policies  of the administration rather than the  previous president that's the reality  but it's better to just not play  political games with this stuff that's  unfortunately how our media and  political system works and there's a  really important thing that I continue  to bring up all of these numbers are  constructions the unemployment number  job growth all that stuff I don't mean  that they are made up that the  statistics are made up but what I mean  is job creation as we measure it GDP  growth as a measure of how the country  is doing it's all a construction we've  decided that these arbitrary measures  tell us how the economy is doing we  accept that they tell us how the economy  is doing it's circular and it has no  sort of independent existence this is  not like a moral relativism or nothing  has any meaning thing it's just that we  decide what tells us how to evaluate the  economy and then we accept that those  are good measures for doing so there is  a really strong case to be made as I've  been making for years that a lot of  these numbers don't really tell us much  about the overall well-being of people  in the country they don't tell us  anything about the damage that we're  doing the country to the planet in terms  of climate change and pollution and the  environment they tell us nothing about  how the mere idea of infinite growth  forever is a destructive and literally  impossible idea because at a certain  point you stretch the planet to the end  of its resources within the context  where we accept that this is sort of how  we evaluate how the economy is doing  these jobs numbers aren't good but more  broadly what I go back to  every month when we get a new report  about expected GDP numbers or job growth  versus expected job growth is that we  have to figure out what the relationship  is going to be of humans to work into  the economy around them because it's  changing very quickly it's not  politically popular to talk about but if  we keep ignoring it  we are going to end up at some point  it's not exactly when in what I guess is  to put it in an understatement sort of  fashion a rude awakening about the world  around us the numbers are bad but we  also have much bigger issues that aren't  being dealt with here
 

